WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:05.160
Hi everyone, in this lesson, we're going to go and take a look at Nanite and how it works

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.900
with our world building when it comes to using Unreal.

3
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.039
So let's get started.

4
00:00:11.039 --> 00:00:13.599
So Nanite is pretty easy to enable.

5
00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:16.800
But one thing you want to do is make sure what items in your scene because you

6
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:22.839
may have pulled them in and forgotten to activate Nanite as they come in, as they're

7
00:00:22.839 --> 00:00:23.839
imported.

8
00:00:23.839 --> 00:00:28.100
But you can check that after the fact once you have items in your scene to be able

9
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.460
to convert what's there.

10
00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.780
It's easily done by right clicking and going to Nanite and clicking on Nanite and you'll

11
00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:41.479
see automatically and it is now enabled in the objects details for rendering.

12
00:00:41.479 --> 00:00:43.679
So say we want to check it really quick.

13
00:00:43.679 --> 00:00:44.679
Let me show you.

14
00:00:44.679 --> 00:00:47.390
Let's go into Unreal.

15
00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:53.210
So here in Unreal, I can go in here and I can type in with my content folder selected,

16
00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:56.340
I can type in Nanite.

17
00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:03.119
You'll see there's enabled and I want to put equal sign equal sign false.

18
00:01:03.119 --> 00:01:06.959
Now this is going to show me everything in my scene that is not Nanite.

19
00:01:06.959 --> 00:01:11.439
I have a couple a few copies in here and a few other things, but I can simply just

20
00:01:11.439 --> 00:01:16.620
select all of these and convert them to a Nanite object.

21
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.260
That's actually pretty convenient.

22
00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.900
Now before we used to have it where you would have to go through the tools and you

23
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:27.299
could like go through debugging used to be a Nanite debugger, but we decided let's

24
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.459
just keep it simple without another window to deal with.

25
00:01:30.459 --> 00:01:35.640
Simply type it into your search content and you'll be able to find all the Nanite objects.

26
00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:41.480
Now the benefit of using Nanite is the fact that again, using it, it is like virtual

27
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:44.980
textures, but for polygons.

28
00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:49.879
It only draws what is needed based on my position in the world on a polygon level

29
00:01:49.879 --> 00:01:53.239
versus an old school LOD level.

30
00:01:53.239 --> 00:01:56.040
Basically LOD polygons, you can think of it that way.

31
00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:01.019
That way there's a less of a change in the silhouette, more accuracy and more detail

32
00:02:01.019 --> 00:02:04.099
shows up on the model as you navigate through the world.

33
00:02:04.099 --> 00:02:07.569
So it's pretty awesome and works great.

34
00:02:07.569 --> 00:02:12.169
What you can see here is we have a Nanite object here and he's actually pretty dense.

35
00:02:12.169 --> 00:02:16.930
I ran a few times to make him a little bit denser and using the modeling tools,

36
00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:23.490
I remeshed him, but this dense object is something that Nanite likes and is able

37
00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:24.909
to calculate it.

38
00:02:24.909 --> 00:02:29.289
Now obviously everything has a particular cap or limit if you keep throwing at it.

39
00:02:29.289 --> 00:02:35.210
So you want to be strategic and you can have all your objects high poly.

40
00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:40.569
That's fine, but for some things that may not be seen very often, if you decide to

41
00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:44.729
make them a little lower poly, Nanite's not going to worry about that so much.

42
00:02:44.729 --> 00:02:47.250
You don't have to worry about it like an old school game, so it kind of breaks that

43
00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:52.409
game thinking, but you do have to be careful about really low poly objects.

44
00:02:52.409 --> 00:02:57.289
And what I mean by that, you'll notice I have a piece of geometry here, which is a

45
00:02:57.289 --> 00:03:00.909
simple continuation of that trim sheet.

46
00:03:00.909 --> 00:03:04.669
I just isolated a region and this is a simple polygon in here.

47
00:03:04.669 --> 00:03:09.189
Now if you have a few of these in your scene, that's fine that you will get less

48
00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:10.789
chances of performance hitting you.

49
00:03:10.789 --> 00:03:15.710
You heard me talk about this earlier, but if you have a lot of simple geometry and

50
00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:20.430
you've converted them to a Nanite object, you could run into some problems.

51
00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:22.150
So just keep that in mind.

52
00:03:22.409 --> 00:03:27.650
If you needed to, you could run the modeling tool and remesh this and make it a higher

53
00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:34.409
poly object if you really do need those flat cards or just in your DCC, go and handle

54
00:03:34.409 --> 00:03:39.930
it there and in your DCC, just go ahead and make it so it's a higher poly flat

55
00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:40.930
plane.

56
00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:43.050
And that actually works just fine too.

57
00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:44.629
So I just wanted to point that out.

58
00:03:44.629 --> 00:03:46.409
Just be careful of those things.

59
00:03:46.409 --> 00:03:49.129
Now there are a few things you do need to keep in mind when it comes to working

60
00:03:49.129 --> 00:03:50.689
with Nanite objects.

61
00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:52.389
Let's pick on the trees for a second here.

62
00:03:52.389 --> 00:03:56.870
We'll get back to them later, but if I'm in a scene and I start to zoom out, you

63
00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:01.750
will notice at times at a particular distance, bushes or plants, the tops of the trees

64
00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:03.110
will disappear.

65
00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:07.789
To prevent this from happening, what you want to do is go into that Nanite object

66
00:04:07.789 --> 00:04:12.469
and you want to go ahead and edit it in such a way so that you are turning on preserved

67
00:04:12.469 --> 00:04:13.469
area.

68
00:04:13.469 --> 00:04:16.389
I'm just going to use this guy still since we still have him open.

69
00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:19.829
His options are still the same, even though he's not a tree.

70
00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:23.620
Let's go ahead and double click on this piece of geometry.

71
00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:25.939
Let me go ahead and pull this piece of geometry into our scene.

72
00:04:25.939 --> 00:04:31.420
I'm going to go full screen.

73
00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:41.019
You'll notice if we type in Nanite and make sure we type it, you'll see we have

74
00:04:41.019 --> 00:04:42.300
a preserved area.

75
00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:46.459
Now again, if your trees are disappearing from a distance, you want to turn this on

76
00:04:46.459 --> 00:04:50.660
and Unreal is nice with the pro tips to tell you, Hey man, this is used for foliage

77
00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:55.420
just to help make sure that these surfaces and everything is working cleanly from a

78
00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:56.740
distance.

79
00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:00.800
Now if you go down below, there's another one which is fairly new called max edge length

80
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:01.939
factor.

81
00:05:01.939 --> 00:05:07.480
This one comes in really handy if you're working with say a construction script and

82
00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:10.920
you're using, say you're making pipes in this particular case.

83
00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:16.579
Let me close this window out for a moment and we're going to go ahead and pick on

84
00:05:16.579 --> 00:05:23.000
this particular piece of geometry and I'm going to double click on the pipes here

85
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:32.910
in my scene and in here we have, if we type in Nanite again, you'll see that

86
00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:36.170
match that max edge length factor.

87
00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:37.750
It is set to 10.

88
00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:43.759
Now for some reason I leave it at default to be say zero and I hit apply.

89
00:05:43.759 --> 00:05:50.639
You're going to notice a degradation or I should say a lower poly version happened

90
00:05:50.639 --> 00:05:54.480
on your pipes unless you're really up close, then they don't look quite as

91
00:05:54.480 --> 00:06:01.740
bad but as soon as you get a far away, those curves become very lower poly and they don't

92
00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:03.529
look so great.

93
00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:09.089
So we made this edge max edge length factor to be able to edit that.

94
00:06:09.089 --> 00:06:13.220
So I'm going to go in here and type in 10 and I'm going to go ahead and hit apply

95
00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:14.660
changes.

96
00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:19.560
This will make it so it's consistent and if I decide to build out from this particular

97
00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:25.300
construction script that I've created, I can actually have less of an issue with that.

98
00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:26.860
And again, you can play with these numbers.

99
00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:30.259
I have mine at 10 because it's working for these pipes.

100
00:06:30.259 --> 00:06:32.740
You could probably get away with maybe five.

101
00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:38.699
That kind of depends on the complexity of your construction of your items and so forth.

102
00:06:38.699 --> 00:06:43.379
Just keep those things in mind when you're building out how you want them to be represented.

103
00:06:43.379 --> 00:06:47.220
Ten seems to work pretty good for this particular pipe construction.

104
00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:50.240
So just keep those things in mind.

105
00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:55.040
Another thing I want to mention too, when it comes to using Nanite, you do want to

106
00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:57.339
be careful when it comes to combining.

107
00:06:57.339 --> 00:07:02.839
You don't want to combine an object in your scene using some of our merge tools.

108
00:07:02.839 --> 00:07:08.600
Now if you decide, you're like, hey, I want to go in here and merge these actors.

109
00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:12.680
You and say they're already Nanite and you go back in there again and you convert

110
00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:14.300
them to Nanite one more time.

111
00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.439
You do got to be careful with that.

112
00:07:16.439 --> 00:07:22.290
If I have, because there can be a double calculation and you may get a performance hit.

113
00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:25.250
You also notice that I have actual rivets here.

114
00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:32.750
Now these little welding rivets, I'm not a plumber, I'm not going to pretend I am,

115
00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:35.589
but these bolts, there you go.

116
00:07:35.589 --> 00:07:39.689
So these bolts in here are pretty low poly.

117
00:07:39.689 --> 00:07:42.509
They're not too low poly, but they're low enough.

118
00:07:42.509 --> 00:07:45.389
And I just brought it in with a model and that's okay.

119
00:07:45.389 --> 00:07:47.910
Nanite's forgiving for very small detail like that.

120
00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:53.790
But if I have a lot of these and I decide like in my scene, I want to merge them and

121
00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:57.629
merge them using the merge tool and convert it to Nanite, you got to be careful because

122
00:07:57.629 --> 00:08:02.949
it's becoming something that wasn't brought in natively, or I should say from the DCC.

123
00:08:02.949 --> 00:08:07.649
You're doing it with Unreal, you're converting Nanite objects, you're merging Nanite objects

124
00:08:07.649 --> 00:08:09.170
and you got to be careful with that.

125
00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:13.670
We go over this more in our optimization for linear content.

126
00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:19.269
But there is an option when it comes to the merge actors, there's an approximate which

127
00:08:19.269 --> 00:08:25.970
allows you to merge Nanite objects, doesn't necessarily mean you should.

128
00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:30.470
So there is a calculation factor that's involved that you got to be careful.

129
00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:32.629
It's better to just have them polyed in.

130
00:08:32.629 --> 00:08:38.009
And as you can see, I can go in here and just kit bash the crap out of things, build

131
00:08:38.009 --> 00:08:41.549
things in pieces, slap them together.

132
00:08:41.549 --> 00:08:46.850
And no one would be the wiser using that whole trim sheet texture that I created for

133
00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:49.169
different aspects and details.

134
00:08:49.169 --> 00:08:53.129
And even simple items like this, these little knobs should be fine.

135
00:08:53.129 --> 00:08:54.610
You just got to be careful.

136
00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:59.210
If you have a lot and you're noticing some performance hit hits happening.

137
00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:03.850
You also have to consider where you're going with your game, whether it's PC, whether

138
00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:06.710
it's console, whether it's a mobile device.

139
00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:10.529
You always want to think smarter as you can when it comes to mobile devices.

140
00:09:10.529 --> 00:09:17.850
You might want to lean more into baking and less of lumen, but all this requires experimentation.

141
00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:21.610
Well that's it with this lecture and the next video we're going to be talking about

142
00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:26.169
blueprints and construction scripts and how to manage your assets as you get them ready

143
00:09:26.169 --> 00:09:29.610
to be calculated for lumen and for Nanite.

144
00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:30.110
